1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:26,000
SPEAKERA: That sounds precise. But even today, we can't say exactly how close he came.

2
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:56,000
SPEAKERA: That's quite a job offer.

3
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:26,000
SPEAKERA: And the post also made him tutor to the future king.

4
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:56,000
SPEAKERB: And his critics called him Beta, meaning second best at everything.

5
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:26,000
SPEAKERB: They had, for a century or so. Aristotle had pointed to the Earth's round shadow on the Moon during an eclipse.

6
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:56,000
SPEAKERB: That only works if the ground curves between the two places.

7
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:26,000
SPEAKERA: Aristotle even reported a size for the Earth, which was four hundred thousand stadia.

8
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:56,000
SPEAKERB: So he needs to measure how different the shadows are, and how far apart the cities are.

9
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:26,000
SPEAKERA: According to Cleomedes, it was read on a scaphe, which is a sundial shaped like a bowl.

10
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:56,000
SPEAKERA: Now comes the key idea. The Sun is so far away that its rays arrive almost parallel.

11
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:26,000
SPEAKERB: Now the Alexandria stick line crosses two parallel lines, which are the ray at Alexandria and the ray down the Syene stick.

12
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:56,000
SPEAKERB: Egypt did have bematists. These were professional pacers who measured land for farming and for taxes.

13
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:26,000
SPEAKERA: Either way, if five thousand stadia is one fiftieth of the way round, the whole way round is fifty times as far.

14
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:56,000
SPEAKERB: Fifty times 5,000 is 250,000 stadia.

15
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:26,000
SPEAKERA: From a shadow and a distance, he had the size of the whole Earth.

16
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:56,000
SPEAKERB: And nobody knows when he wrote it, but it was somewhere between the first century BC and about AD 400.

17
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:26,000
SPEAKERA: Two hundred and fifty-two thousand divides evenly by every whole number from one to ten, but two hundred and fifty thousand doesn't.

18
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:56,000
SPEAKERA: That would make the unit itself part of the puzzle.

19
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:26,000
SPEAKERB: That's a spread of 40 percent.

20
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:56,000
SPEAKERB: With a stadion of about 176 metres, he's about 10 percent too big.
